EXAM_DATA.papers["2026-english-one"] = {"id":"2026-english-one","year":2026,"label":"2026 英语一","era":"modern","sourceUrl":"https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/kaoyan/paper/2026-english-one/","timeLimitMin":180,"scoreMode":"estimated","parts":[{"id":"section1","kind":"cloze","title":"Section I 完形填空","directions":"Directions: Read the following text. Choose the best word(s) for each numbered blank and mark A, B, C or D on the ANSWER SHEET . (10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section1-1","text":"Advances in artificial intelligence (AI) are rapidly changing every aspect of human life. The world of AI is buzzing with an exciting potential to improve and enrich our lives.   {{1}}   , AI also has the potential hazard of   {{2}}   our experiences in ways we might find difficult to control. One such   {{3}}   is how we understand and experience beauty."},{"id":"p-section1-2","text":"AI can be a collaborative tool in a wide range of creative endeavors.   {{4}}   human creativity and AI algorithms can lead to unique artistic   {{5}}   that are beautiful to the human eye. These collaborations are likely to become increasingly common.   {{6}}   , as convenient and provocative, AI enables virtual try-on experiences where you can virtually   {{7}}   makeup, hairstyles, clothing, and even cosmetic procedures   {{8}}   making any physical changes. Individuals can now experiment with different looks and   {{9}}   their preferences, potentially expanding the range of beauty ideals."},{"id":"p-section1-3","text":"AI algorithms can   {{10}}   facial features and skin conditions to provide personalized beauty recommendations. This   {{11}}   approach aims to cater to individual preferences and enhance the concept of beauty tailored to each person's unique characteristics.   {{12}}   , AI can be a fun vehicle for self-discovery."},{"id":"p-section1-4","text":"While AI offers exciting possibilities, it also raises ethical   {{13}}   . There is a risk of deepening societal beauty   {{14}}   and perpetuating unattainable beauty standards.   {{15}}   , AI-powered beauty filters and editing tools can lead to distorted self-perception and   {{16}}   body dissatisfaction. As summarized in a recent post on \"The Hidden Dangers of Online Beauty Filters\",   {{17}}   on this technology for social presentation can cause harm   {{18}}   body image issues, lower self-esteem, and social anxiety."},{"id":"p-section1-5","text":"It's important to note that while AI can enhance our   {{19}}   of beauty, it should not   {{20}}   the genuine human experience and the emotional connections we derive from seeing the beauty in each other."}]},"questions":[{"n":1,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Still"},{"key":"B","text":"Therefore"},{"key":"C","text":"Afterward"},{"key":"D","text":"Instead"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"1.","reason":"前句赞美、后句揭隐患——典型转折 线索是相邻两句的语义对立：前句 The world of AI is buzzing with an exciting potential to improve and enrich our lives. 是正面铺垫；后句 AI also has the potential hazard of __(2)__ our experiences in ways we might find difficult to control. 是反面提醒。中间需要的是一个\"尽管前面说了好处、但仍要看到隐患\"的让步式转折，而不是因果或时序。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"Still 作副词意为\"尽管如此、仍然\"，专门用于承认前句之后再亮出反面，与 AI also has the potential hazard 中 also 的\"另一面\"语气彼此呼应，是最贴合上下文的让步转折。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"Therefore 表\"因此\"，要求前句是后句的原因。但\"AI 有令人激动的潜力\"显然推不出\"AI 因此有主导我们体验的隐患\"，逻辑指向相反，错。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"Afterward 是\"之后、随后\"，纯时间先后。原文两句不是\"先发生 A 再发生 B\"的时序关系，而是同时存在的正反两面，时序词放进去既不通顺也不照应 also。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"Instead 意为\"反而、而不是\"，会把前句整个否定掉。但后句用 also 明确承认\"AI 也有好处\"，并非要推翻前句，所以 Instead 的替代/对立太重，错。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":2,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"reviewing"},{"key":"B","text":"narrating"},{"key":"C","text":"ignoring"},{"key":"D","text":"dominating"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"2.","reason":"hazard + difficult to control 锁定\"强势主导\"义 本句 AI also has the potential hazard of __(2)__ our experiences in ways we might find difficult to control. 中，hazard 表明是隐患，difficult to control 表明 AI 对体验有一种压倒性的、人难以反制的作用，因此空位要填的是\"主导、支配\"一类的强动作，而不是温和的\"看、讲、忽略\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"reviewing 是\"回顾、审查\"，是一种温和的回看动作，与 hazard、difficult to control 所要求的\"压倒性\"色彩明显不符。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"narrating 是\"叙述、讲述\"，搭配 our experiences 充其量是\"讲我们的体验\"，没有主导/控制含义，也不构成隐患，与上下文不匹配。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"ignoring 是\"忽视\"。AI 忽视我们的体验，恰恰意味着对体验没有干预，与\"以我们难以控制的方式作用于体验\"的语义方向相反。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"dominating 是\"支配、主宰\"，对应 AI 把我们的体验\"以难以控制的方式\"接管，与 hazard 形成自然搭配，是四项中唯一兼具\"强动作\"和\"负面色彩\"的词。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":3,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"reason"},{"key":"B","text":"area"},{"key":"C","text":"clue"},{"key":"D","text":"belief"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"3.","reason":"上一句提到的是\"难以控制的体验范围\" 上句 AI also has the potential hazard of __(2)__ our experiences in ways we might find difficult to control. 谈到 AI 可能主导的\"experiences\"，而本句把例子缩到 how we understand and experience beauty，是把\"体验\"的一个具体分支拎出来——这是一种\"范围/领域\"上的细化。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"reason 要求下文给出一个\"为什么\"。但 how we understand and experience beauty 是一个领域，而非\"AI 主导体验的原因\"，逻辑不匹配。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"area 表\"领域、方面\"，正好把上文宽泛的 experiences 收束为一个具体子领域：审美。下一句用 such 引出审美作为该 area 的例子，结构完全对得上。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"clue 是\"线索、提示\"，要求下文是某个待破解问题的线索。原文是在论证 AI 的影响范围，不是在破案找线索，词义不搭。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"belief 是\"信念、看法\"。但 how we understand and experience beauty 是一种行为/活动范畴，而不是一种被持有的观念，与 belief 的指称不一致。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":4,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Balancing"},{"key":"B","text":"Distinguishing"},{"key":"C","text":"Combining"},{"key":"D","text":"Introducing"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"4.","reason":"上一句的 collaborative 已经给出方向 上一句 AI can be a collaborative tool in a wide range of creative endeavors. 把 AI 定性为\"协作工具\"，本句是其展开：人和 AI 一起做事，产出 unique artistic __(5)__ that are beautiful to the human eye，需要的动作显然是\"结合\"而非\"区分\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"Balancing 强调在两者间求平衡、避免某一方过强，但本句要表达的是两者一起产生新作品，不是\"维持均势\"，与 collaborative 的合作含义错位。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"Distinguishing 是\"区分、辨别\"，意味着把两者分开，与协作完全相反，被快速排除。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"Combining 是\"结合、联合\"，与上一句 collaborative tool 形成同义重申：把人类创造力与 AI 算法结合起来，产生独特的艺术成果，最贴合上下文。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"Introducing 是\"引入、介绍\"，通常是\"把 A 引入到 B 里\"，结构上要 A to B；这里直接接两个并列名词，不构成 introducing 的常见用法，也没有\"双方共同产生作品\"的含义。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":5,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"prospects"},{"key":"B","text":"outcomes"},{"key":"C","text":"ambitions"},{"key":"D","text":"sentiments"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"5.","reason":"lead to + beautiful to the human eye 锁定\"产物\"义 本句 __(4)__ human creativity and AI algorithms can lead to unique artistic __(5)__ that are beautiful to the human eye. 中 lead to 暗示是一种结果产物，beautiful to the human eye 进一步要求这种产物是可被肉眼欣赏的，因此空必须填\"成果、产物\"类词。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"prospects 指\"前景、可能性\"，是对未来的展望，无法被 beautiful to the human eye 直接欣赏，与定语冲突。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"outcomes 表\"结果、成果\"，与 lead to 天然搭配，且可指具体的艺术作品本身，恰好能被 beautiful to the human eye 修饰，最为契合。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"ambitions 是\"雄心、抱负\"，属内心动机，没有可观赏性，与 beautiful to the human eye 不构成搭配。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"sentiments 是\"情感、情绪\"，描述的是看作品的人之内心感受，不是被看的对象本身；说\"艺术情感对人眼是美的\"在逻辑层面错位。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":6,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"At first"},{"key":"B","text":"By comparison"},{"key":"C","text":"For instance"},{"key":"D","text":"In general"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"6.","reason":"上句给\"艺术协作\"作例，本句给\"虚拟试妆\"作另一例 上一组例子是 __(4)__ human creativity and AI algorithms can lead to unique artistic __(5)__ 与 These collaborations are likely to become increasingly common.——讲 AI 的艺术协作；本句换到 AI enables virtual try-on experiences where you can virtually __(7)__ makeup, hairstyles, clothing, and even cosmetic procedures——同样是 AI 在创意领域的应用，但换了一个面向用户的具体例子，属于\"另举一例\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"At first 表\"起初\"，要求后面有\"后来怎样\"的转折，但本段没有时间先后的暗示，逻辑不成立。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"By comparison 用来引出有反差的对比，但艺术协作与虚拟试妆都是 AI 的正面用途，没有反差对立，错。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"For instance 表\"例如\"，正好引出与艺术协作并列的另一个 AI 应用例子——虚拟试妆，与全段\"列举 AI 在创意领域好处\"的结构高度一致。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"In general 通常用于做总括/概括，本句却是给一个新的具体例子，方向相反。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":7,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"test"},{"key":"B","text":"copy"},{"key":"C","text":"link"},{"key":"D","text":"save"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"7.","reason":"try-on + virtually 锁定\"试\"义 virtual try-on experiences 已经直接给出\"试戴/试穿\"的概念，try 本就是\"试\"的核心词，空位动词显然要呼应这一概念，且后接的宾语都是可以\"试用\"的对象——makeup、hairstyle、clothing。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"test 表\"试用、试戴\"，与 try-on 同义，正好搭配 makeup、hairstyles、clothing，是最自然、最贴合的选择。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"copy 是\"复制、模仿\"。用户不是在虚拟环境里\"复制\"妆容/发型，而是\"试用\"，且 copy 与 try-on 并不同义，方向不对。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"link 是\"连接、关联\"，与 makeup、hairstyles 的常规搭配毫无关联，更与 try-on 概念不沾边。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"save 是\"保存\"，通常发生在\"先试用后保存\"的最后一步，而本句要表达的是\"试用\"这个动作本身，明显错位。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":8,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"upon"},{"key":"B","text":"beyond"},{"key":"C","text":"through"},{"key":"D","text":"before"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"8.","reason":"virtual try-on 与 physical changes 是\"试在前、改在后\" AI enables virtual try-on experiences where you can virtually __(7)__ makeup, hairstyles, clothing, and even cosmetic procedures __(8)__ making any physical changes. 中，virtual try-on 是\"先在虚拟中试\"，physical changes 是\"真做出改变\"，两者之间的时间关系是\"虚拟试用发生在物理改变之前\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"upon 接动名词常表\"一……就……\"，意味着两个动作几乎同时发生；但本句强调的是\"还没做实体改变之前就先虚拟试\"，并非紧接同时，方向不对。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"beyond 表\"超出、超越\"，会被读作\"在做实物改变之外（还）虚拟试\"，但全文重点不是\"两件事都做\"，而是\"不做实体改变也能试\"，语义不贴。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"through 表\"通过、借助\"，会变成\"通过做实物改变来虚拟试\"，与上下文\"无须实物改变\"完全相反。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"before 表\"在……之前\"，完美匹配\"在做出任何实物改变之前先在虚拟里试\"的逻辑，是 AI 试妆类应用最常见的卖点。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":9,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"explore"},{"key":"B","text":"recall"},{"key":"C","text":"simplify"},{"key":"D","text":"cherish"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"9.","reason":"experiment + expanding the range 共指\"探索\"义 Individuals can now experiment with different looks and __(9)__ their preferences, potentially expanding the range of beauty ideals. 中 experiment 暗示\"尝试不同\"，后置短语 expanding the range of beauty ideals 暗示\"拓宽、发现新偏好\"，因此空位动词与 explore 同义最贴。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"explore 表\"探索、发掘\"，与 experiment 形成同义并列，又与 expanding the range of beauty ideals 形成因果延伸——通过探索个人偏好来拓宽审美范围。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"recall 是\"回忆\"，对应的是\"已知的、过去的\"偏好；本句要的是\"通过试用发现新的偏好\"，方向相反。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"simplify 是\"简化\"，与最后一句的 expanding the range 直接矛盾，错。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"cherish 是\"珍惜、珍视\"，强调情感而非通过试用得到结果，与 experiment 不构成同义并列。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":10,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"recover"},{"key":"B","text":"arrange"},{"key":"C","text":"reserve"},{"key":"D","text":"analyze"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"10.","reason":"personalized recommendations 锁定 analyze 系动作 to provide personalized beauty recommendations 中\"个性化建议\"必须以对个体数据的分析为前提，AI 算法的标志性动作恰是 analyze / process / model 这类\"分析处理\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"recover 是\"恢复、找回\"，与正常的 facial features、skin conditions 不构成搭配——这些不是要被\"恢复\"的对象，错。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"arrange 是\"安排、布置\"，对象多为事件、物品；说\"算法安排面部特征\"在常识上不通。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"reserve 是\"预订、保留\"，常搭配座位、资源等，与\"面部特征\"完全不搭。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"analyze 是\"分析\"，与 facial features、skin conditions 自然搭配，又能直接通往 personalized recommendations，是 AI 类主语的标准动词。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":11,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"localized"},{"key":"B","text":"normalized"},{"key":"C","text":"randomized"},{"key":"D","text":"customized"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"11.","reason":"personalized + tailored 双重锁定\"定制化\"义 上句 personalized beauty recommendations 与下句 tailored to each person's unique characteristics 都强调\"为每个人量身定制\"，本句的空形容词必须与之同义。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"localized 是\"本地化的、局部的\"，强调地域适配，与\"每个人独特特征\"无关。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"normalized 是\"标准化、规范化的\"，意味着把差异抹平，与\"为每个人量身定制\"恰好相反。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"randomized 是\"随机化的\"，与本段\"基于个人数据精准推荐\"的语义完全冲突。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"customized 是\"量身定制的\"，与 personalized、tailored 三词同义连用，组成本段的标志性主题串，最贴合。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":12,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"At best"},{"key":"B","text":"To the contrary"},{"key":"C","text":"By definition"},{"key":"D","text":"In this way"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"12.","reason":"前文铺垫已具备\"个性化分析→自我发现\"的因果链 段内已说明 AI algorithms can __(10)__ facial features and skin conditions to provide personalized beauty recommendations. 与 enhance the concept of beauty tailored to each person's unique characteristics——这些行为天然指向\"借助 AI 探索自我\"，本句作为段尾结论，需用\"如此一来\"类副词承接。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"At best 表\"充其量、最多也就是\"，带轻微贬抑，会把\"AI 是自我探索的有趣载体\"变成\"AI 顶多只是\"，与积极论调不符。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"To the contrary 表\"恰恰相反\"，要求前文有相反观点；但段内并未给出可反驳的观点，逻辑不成立。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"By definition 多用于\"从定义上来说\"，要求后面的内容是某个概念的定义结果；但\"AI 是自我探索的有趣载体\"并非任何概念的定义衍生，错。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"In this way 表\"如此一来、以这种方式\"，正好把前文\"个性化分析→定制化建议\"的过程承接为\"由此 AI 成为自我探索的工具\"，是段尾总结最自然的承接。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":13,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"divisions"},{"key":"B","text":"expectations"},{"key":"C","text":"concerns"},{"key":"D","text":"values"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"13.","reason":"while + raises 锁定\"伦理担忧\"义 本句 While AI offers exciting possibilities, it also raises ethical __(13)__. 中 while 表让步，紧接的是负面信息；raises 又是\"引发\"，二者合起来要求一个\"负面 + 待解决\"的名词。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"divisions 是\"分歧、分裂\"，更多用作 social divisions、party divisions，少与 ethical 搭，且 raise 这类\"引出\"动词更常与 concerns 搭。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"expectations 是\"期待\"，整体偏正面，与 while 让步引出负面信息的方向相反。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"concerns 是\"担忧、关切\"，raise ethical concerns 是固定搭配级高频组合，正好对应 while 之后引出的负面/待审思一面。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"values 是\"价值观\"，raise ethical values 在英文中并非常规搭配；价值观本就存在，不是被\"引发\"出来的，错。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":14,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"pressures"},{"key":"B","text":"mysteries"},{"key":"C","text":"understandings"},{"key":"D","text":"suspicions"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"14.","reason":"perpetuating unattainable beauty standards 锁定\"审美压力\" perpetuating unattainable beauty standards——\"使无法达成的审美标准长期化\"，正是\"社会审美压力\"的典型描述；与之并列的空名词需要表达\"社会层面对美的压力\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"pressures 是\"压力\"，societal beauty pressures 正好表达\"社会层面的审美压力\"，与 perpetuating unattainable beauty standards 形成因果同向，最为贴合。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"mysteries 是\"神秘\"，与 risk、deepening 的负面方向不符；说\"加剧社会审美神秘\"在语义上也很奇怪。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"understandings 是\"理解\"，加剧一种\"理解\"在英语里通常是积极的过程，与 risk 的负面定调相反。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"suspicions 是\"怀疑\"，与 unattainable beauty standards 难以构成同向延伸——\"怀疑\"不会被审美标准的难以达成所同向加剧。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":15,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Approximately"},{"key":"B","text":"Additionally"},{"key":"C","text":"Alternatively"},{"key":"D","text":"Accidentally"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"15.","reason":"上下两句都是\"AI 带来负面效应\"的并列 上句 There is a risk of deepening societal beauty __(14)__ and perpetuating unattainable beauty standards. 与本句 AI-powered beauty filters and editing tools can lead to distorted self-perception and __(16)__ body dissatisfaction. 都是 AI 在审美上的负面影响的具体表现，是并列展开。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"Approximately 是\"大约\"，用于数字近似估计，与句间逻辑承接无关。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"Additionally 是\"此外\"，正好把第二个负面例子（滤镜导致自我认知扭曲）添加到上一例（社会审美压力）之后，是并列展开的标准承接。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"Alternatively 是\"或者\"，要求前后是可替代的两条路径；但前后两句不是\"二选一\"，而是\"两者都有\"，逻辑不对。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"Accidentally 是\"偶然\"，但文中所讲的滤镜导致扭曲自我认知是系统性现象，不是偶发事故，错。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":16,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"deal with"},{"key":"B","text":"result from"},{"key":"C","text":"contribute to"},{"key":"D","text":"focus on"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"16.","reason":"并列 distorted self-perception 决定方向 can lead to distorted self-perception and __(16)__ body dissatisfaction 中前一个并列项的核心动作是 lead to（导致），空位短语必须与之同向同时态，表\"导致/加剧 body dissatisfaction\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"deal with 是\"处理、应对\"，言下之意是把问题处理掉，与 lead to 完全相反方向，错。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"result from 是\"源于、由……引起\"，会把因果颠倒为\"AI 工具源于身体不满\"，与上下文逻辑相反。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"contribute to 是\"导致、加剧\"，与并列的 lead to 完全同向，正好串成\"AI 工具会扭曲自我认知并加剧身体不满\"。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"focus on 是\"聚焦于、关注\"，并不构成因果关系，与 lead to 的并列结构错位。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":17,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"starting"},{"key":"B","text":"checking"},{"key":"C","text":"relying"},{"key":"D","text":"working"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"17.","reason":"for social presentation + cause harm 锁定\"依赖\"义 __(17)__ on this technology for social presentation can cause harm __(18)__ body image issues, lower self-esteem, and social anxiety. 中 for social presentation 表\"用于社交呈现\"，cause harm 表负面后果——人因\"依赖\"该技术做社交呈现而受伤，relying on 与上下文完美闭环。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"starting 是\"开始\"，starting on 不构成稳定搭配，且\"开始使用某技术\"本身并不必然 cause harm，逻辑承担不起。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"checking 是\"检查\"，checking on technology 含\"核查技术状态\"之意，与社交场景和负面后果不搭。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"relying 与 on 形成 rely on（依赖），正好对应\"过度依赖该技术做社交呈现导致危害\"的标准逻辑，与第 4 段\"伦理隐忧\"主线契合。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"working working on technology 多表\"研究/开发技术\"，主语通常是开发者；本句主语是\"普通人将技术用于社交\"，错位。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":18,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"apart from"},{"key":"B","text":"such as"},{"key":"C","text":"regardless of"},{"key":"D","text":"prior to"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"18.","reason":"harm 与三类问题构成\"总-分\"举例 harm __(18)__ body image issues, lower self-esteem, and social anxiety 中 harm 是上位概括词，body image issues、lower self-esteem、social anxiety 是三类具体危害——总-分关系，需要的是 such as。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"apart from 是\"除……之外\"，会被读成\"除身体形象问题之外的伤害\"，但作者意图是\"列举伤害的几类\"，不是排除某些。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"such as 是\"例如\"，正好引出 harm 的具体例子——身体形象问题、自尊降低、社交焦虑，与上下文结构完全一致。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"regardless of 是\"不论、无视\"，与列举性的总-分结构毫无关联，错。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"prior to 是\"在……之前\"，时间义，与列举性的总-分结构无关。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":19,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"imitation"},{"key":"B","text":"appreciation"},{"key":"C","text":"preservation"},{"key":"D","text":"consumption"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"19.","reason":"下文 emotional connections + seeing the beauty 锁定\"欣赏\" it should not __(20)__ the genuine human experience and the emotional connections we derive from seeing the beauty in each other. 中\"看到彼此身上的美\"\"由此产生的情感连接\"明显指向\"欣赏\"——对美的感知和体验，因此空名词应是 appreciation。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"imitation 是\"模仿\"，指对美的复制，与下文\"genuine human experience\"\"emotional connections\"对不上号。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"appreciation 是\"欣赏\"，appreciation of beauty 是经典搭配，与下文\"由欣赏美而生的情感连接\"完美闭环，且与 enhance 这一动词的正面色彩一致。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"preservation 是\"保存、保护\"，preservation of beauty 偏向\"保护美\"（如文物、自然），与\"对美的感受\"不在一个层面。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"consumption 是\"消费\"，consumption of beauty 偏功利、商业化色彩，与下文\"genuine\"\"emotional\"等正面词的方向冲突。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":20,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"replace"},{"key":"B","text":"seize"},{"key":"C","text":"share"},{"key":"D","text":"reflect"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"20.","reason":"while enhance … should not __ 锁定\"取代\"义 while AI can enhance our __(19)__ of beauty, it should not __(20)__ the genuine human experience 中 enhance 与 should not __ 形成\"辅助 vs 取代\"的对照，正好对应作者立场——AI 可以辅助、但不可越界替代真正的人类体验。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"replace 是\"取代、替代\"，与上半句 enhance 形成强烈对照，直接落出全文核心立场：AI 可增强体验、但不应取代真实的人类体验。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"seize 是\"抓住、夺取\"，与 the genuine human experience 不构成自然搭配，且语义太具体物，错位。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"share 是\"分享\"，与 enhance 在方向上不冲突，无法形成 while-should not 的转折对照，错。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"reflect 是\"反映、映照\"，把它代入会变成\"AI 不应反映真实的人类体验\"，与作者要警示\"取代\"风险的意图相反。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a","kind":"reading","title":"Text 1","textNo":1,"directions":"Directions: Read the following four texts. Answer the questions after each text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET . (40 points)","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 1","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1","text":"For thousands of years, donkeys have been critical for propelling human civilizations forward. They've helped pull wheeled vehicles, carry travelers and move goods across the world. But where and when these animals first became intertwined with humans has been a mystery. Now, researchers have used genomes of over 200 donkeys to trace their domestication back to a single event around 7,000 years ago in East Africa — about 3,000 years before humans tamed horses. The team published their findings in the journal Science this month."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2","text":"\"Through their DNA, the animals are telling their history themselves,\" co-author Samantha Brooks, an equine researcher at the University of Florida, says in a statement. \"We usually only get the human's side of history through written accounts, but of course written history does not always record exactly how something happened. Looking at these DNA sequences, we get a biological testimony to the environment these animals lived in and the experiences they survived.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3","text":"The researchers examined 207 genomes from modern donkeys living in 31 countries across the globe. They also looked at genomes from 15 wild equids and 31 earlier donkeys that lived between about 4,000 and 100 years ago. The team reconstructed the animals' evolutionary tree and used computer models to pinpoint the domestication event when herders in Kenya and the Horn of Africa tamed wild asses. They then traced how the animals spread across the rest of the continent into Europe and Asia about 2,500 years later."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4","text":"Though it's still unclear why the original domestication happened, Science News' Freda Kreier reports that the event coincided with the Sahara growing larger and drier. \"Donkeys are champions when it comes to carrying stuff and are good at crossing deserts,\" co-author Ludovic Orlando, an evolutionary biologist at Paul Sabatier University in France, tells the publication. Prehistoric humans may have tamed donkeys to help navigate the expanding Sahara."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-5","text":"Researchers say these findings could help put donkeys in the spotlight. The animals could benefit from more research: Currently, there are no published genomes from donkeys located south of the Equator in Africa. But understanding where the animals were first domesticated could guide archaeologists to a narrow region to search for insights about the original tamed donkeys."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-6","text":"Not only does understanding the equines' genetic makeup help reveal their contribution to human history, but it also might improve their management in the future, as climate change alters the planet's environment, write the authors."}]},"questions":[{"n":21,"stem":"What can be learned about donkeys from Paragraph 1?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"They seemed mysterious to human ancestors."},{"key":"B","text":"They underwent multiple domestication events."},{"key":"C","text":"They were tamed at an earlier time than horses."},{"key":"D","text":"They were vividly portrayed by ancient travelers."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"从第一段可以了解到关于驴的什么信息?","choicesZh":{"A":"它们对人类祖先来说似乎很神秘。","B":"它们经历了多次驯化事件。","C":"它们被驯化的时间早于马。","D":"它们被古代旅行者生动地描绘过。"},"analysis":{"type":"What can be learned about donkeys from Paragraph 1?问的是从指定段落能获取的事实信息，没有 infer/imply/suggest 之类的推断信号词。","parse":"What can be learned about donkeys from Paragraph 1?为一般疑问转化的特殊疑问句，主干 What can be learned... about donkeys 是被动语态，逻辑主语隐含为「读者」；from Paragraph 1 是状语限定信息来源。无陷阱。","locateEn":"Now, researchers have used genomes of over 200 donkeys to trace their domestication back to a single event around 7,000 years ago in East Africa — about 3,000 years before humans tamed horses.","locateZh":"如今，研究者用 200 多份驴基因组将其驯化追溯到约 7000 年前东非的一次单一事件——比人类驯化马早约 3000 年。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文But where and when these animals first became intertwined with humans has been a mystery说的是驯化的时空对人类「是个谜」，并非驴对人类祖先显得神秘；选项把「研究未解的问题」偷换为「驴本身的形象」。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文trace their domestication back to a single event明确为「单一一次驯化事件」，选项称 multiple domestication events，正话反说。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判「驴比马更早被驯化」与They were tamed at an earlier time than horses在比较对象（horses）与方向（earlier）上完全对接，无歧义。","pairs":[{"orig":"about 3,000 years before humans tamed horses","opt":"They were tamed at an earlier time than horses"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"第 1 段提到 carry travelers，但全文无任何「古代旅行者生动描绘驴」的内容；选项把「驴载旅行者」改造成「旅行者画驴」，凭空捏造行为方向。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":22,"stem":"What message is conveyed in Brooks' statement?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The earliest habitats of donkeys are hardly traceable."},{"key":"B","text":"It is increasingly easy to read donkeys' DNA sequences."},{"key":"C","text":"Written accounts contain vital clues for donkey research."},{"key":"D","text":"Genetic analysis offers insight into the history of donkeys."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"Brooks的陈述传达了什么信息?","choicesZh":{"A":"驴的最早栖息地几乎无法追溯。","B":"读取驴的DNA序列变得越来越容易。","C":"文字记载包含驴研究的重要线索。","D":"基因分析为驴的历史提供了见解。"},"analysis":{"type":"What message is conveyed in Brooks' statement?问的是引语本身传达的信息，是对引语内容的同义概括，不要求跨段推断。","parse":"What message is conveyed in Brooks' statement?主干 What message is conveyed，被动语态；in Brooks' statement 是地点状语，限定信息出处。所有格 Brooks' 容易被基础薄弱考生误读为复数，注意是单数所有格。","locateEn":"Through their DNA, the animals are telling their history themselves. ... Looking at these DNA sequences, we get a biological testimony to the environment these animals lived in and the experiences they survived.","locateZh":"通过它们的 DNA，动物们自己在讲述自己的历史。……审视这些 DNA 序列，我们获得了关于这些动物所处环境与所历经历的生物学证词。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Brooks 引语没有谈及驴的「最早栖息地难以追溯」；本段是讲 DNA 能讲述历史，而非讲历史多么难讲。选项把全文背景（驯化时空一度是 mystery）拿来冒充 Brooks 的观点。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"Brooks 只说通过 DNA 可获洞见，并未提及「读 DNA 越来越容易」；选项的「increasingly easy」是凭空添加的趋势判断。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Brooks 明确说written history does not always record exactly how something happened——文字记载不一定准确；选项却称「文字记载含关键线索」，恰恰反着说。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判「DNA 揭示驴的历史」与Genetic analysis offers insight into the history of donkeys在工具（DNA/Genetic analysis）、动作（揭示/offer insight）、对象（history）上完全对应。","pairs":[{"orig":"Through their DNA, the animals are telling their history themselves","opt":"Genetic analysis offers insight into the history of donkeys"},{"orig":"we get a biological testimony to the environment these animals lived in and the experiences they survived","opt":"Genetic analysis offers insight into the history of donkeys"}]}}}}},{"n":23,"stem":"In their study, the researchers investigated how donkeys ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"dispersed widely in the world."},{"key":"B","text":"survived with the help of herders."},{"key":"C","text":"developed certain behavioral traits."},{"key":"D","text":"adapted to the changing environment."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"在他们的研究中,研究人员调查了驴是如何____","choicesZh":{"A":"在世界各地广泛分散的。","B":"在牧民的帮助下存活下来的。","C":"发展出某些行为特征的。","D":"适应不断变化的环境的。"},"analysis":{"type":"Q23是不完整陈述句，选项作句子延续，问的是研究的客观内容，本质是对方法段一句话的同义改写。","parse":"In their study, the researchers investigated how donkeys ____主干 the researchers investigated how donkeys 后跟动词残缺；how 引导宾语从句，选项是该从句的谓语部分。无陷阱。","locateEn":"They then traced how the animals spread across the rest of the continent into Europe and Asia about 2,500 years later.","locateZh":"随后他们追踪了动物如何在约 2500 年后向非洲其他地区扩散，并进入欧洲与亚洲。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判「驴如何向全球扩散」与dispersed widely in the world在动作（扩散）与范围（全球）上一一对应。","pairs":[{"orig":"spread across the rest of the continent into Europe and Asia","opt":"dispersed widely in the world"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文 herders 出现在描述「肯尼亚与非洲之角的牧民驯服野驴」处（herders in Kenya and the Horn of Africa tamed wild asses），是驯化场景；选项却说「在牧民帮助下生存」，把「被驯化」改写为「靠牧民活下去」，对象关系完全错位。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"全文未提及任何驴的「行为特征/习性」研究；第 3 段仅讲基因组、演化树、扩散路径，没有 behavioral traits 这条线。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"第 4 段虽提到撒哈拉扩张与驴驯化同期，但那是 Orlando 对「为什么驯化」的推测，不是「研究者实际研究的内容」；选项把 Q24 的内容偷渡到 Q23，越界推断。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":24,"stem":"As to why the original domestication of donkeys happened, Orlando ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"challenges conventional ideas."},{"key":"B","text":"provides a possible explanation."},{"key":"C","text":"calls for evidence from the Sahara."},{"key":"D","text":"holds a different view from Kreier."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"关于驴最初驯化发生的原因,Orlando____","choicesZh":{"A":"挑战传统观念。","B":"提供了一种可能的解释。","C":"呼吁来自撒哈拉的证据。","D":"持有与Kreier不同的观点。"},"analysis":{"type":"As to why the original domestication of donkeys happened, Orlando ____问 Orlando 关于「为什么」做了什么动作（解释/反驳/质疑），是对该人物言论性质的客观判定。","parse":"As to why the original domestication of donkeys happened, Orlando ____中 As to why... 是介词短语作状语（=regarding the question of why），主干 Orlando ____，谓语残缺由选项补。As to = regarding，等同 about，不是「至于做某事」。","locateEn":"\"Donkeys are champions when it comes to carrying stuff and are good at crossing deserts,\" co-author Ludovic Orlando, an evolutionary biologist at Paul Sabatier University in France, tells the publication. Prehistoric humans may have tamed donkeys to help navigate the expanding Sahara.","locateZh":"「驴在驮物方面是冠军，且擅长穿越沙漠」，合作者、法国保罗·萨巴蒂耶大学进化生物学家 Orlando 告诉该刊。史前人类可能驯化驴是为了帮助穿越扩张中的撒哈拉。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Orlando 并未挑战任何「传统观点」，原文也没有提到所谓「传统观点」存在；他只是顺着「为何驯化」这个公开未解的问题给出一种可能解释。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判「Orlando 给出可能解释」与provides a possible explanation在动作（提供）、性质（可能/解释）上完全吻合。","pairs":[{"orig":"Prehistoric humans may have tamed donkeys to help navigate the expanding Sahara","opt":"provides a possible explanation"},{"orig":"Donkeys are champions when it comes to carrying stuff and are good at crossing deserts","opt":"provides a possible explanation"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Orlando 没有呼吁任何「从撒哈拉获取证据」；他只是提到撒哈拉扩张这一同期背景，作为推测的合理化情境。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Kreier 是转述 Orlando 观点的记者（Science News' Freda Kreier reports that），与 Orlando 之间无任何观点对立；选项把「报道与被报道」的关系误读为「观点对立」。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":25,"stem":"The authors think that their research could help with ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"greater protection of wildlife."},{"key":"B","text":"better management of donkeys."},{"key":"C","text":"recovering early types of donkeys."},{"key":"D","text":"raising awareness of climate change."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"作者认为他们的研究可以有助于____","choicesZh":{"A":"更好地保护野生动物。","B":"更好地管理驴。","C":"恢复早期类型的驴。","D":"提高对气候变化的认识。"},"analysis":{"type":"The authors think that their research could help with ____问的是作者明确表述的研究用途，是对原文一句话的同义替换。","parse":"The authors think that their research could help with ____主干 The authors think + that 宾语从句，从句主语 their research，谓语 could help with（情态动词表可能性，与原文 might 对应）；help with 后接名词性宾语，由选项补足。无陷阱。","locateEn":"Not only does understanding the equines' genetic makeup help reveal their contribution to human history, but it also might improve their management in the future, as climate change alters the planet's environment, write the authors.","locateZh":"作者写道，理解这些马科动物的遗传构成不仅有助于揭示它们对人类历史的贡献，还可能在气候变化改变全球环境之际改善对它们的管理。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文讲的是改善对驴（已被驯化的家畜）的管理，而非保护「野生动物」；选项把家畜 donkeys 偷换为 wildlife，对象类别完全错位。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判「改善对驴的管理」与better management of donkeys在动作（改善/变好）与对象（驴的管理）上完全对接。","pairs":[{"orig":"it also might improve their management in the future","opt":"better management of donkeys"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"第 5 段提到了解最初驯化地能帮助考古学家寻找「最早被驯化驴的线索」，但只是「寻找线索」，并非「恢复早期驴的类型」；选项把搜寻线索升级为「恢复/复原物种」，越界过度。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文 climate change 是「驴管理需要适应的外部背景」（as climate change alters the planet's environment），并非研究要「提升对其的认识」；选项把背景条件错当成研究目标。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-2","kind":"reading","title":"Text 2","textNo":2,"directions":"Directions: Read the following four texts. Answer the questions after each text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET . (40 points)","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 2","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-1","text":"There's no business like show business — but in Los Angeles, it feels like there's no business at all."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-2","text":"If that sounds melodramatic, consider this: The Art Directors Guild, a labor union representing about 3,000 film workers, has suspended a training program and issued a statement explaining that \"we cannot in good conscience encourage you to pursue our profession.\" This is a reaction to Hollywood's decline, which is reaching a critical point for the industry and Southern California."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-3","text":"Production has been slipping away from Hollywood since the 1950s, but the effects have never been more apparent than at present. Other regions in the United States, Canada and Europe have steadily increased incentives to attract TV shows and movies, leaving California in the dust. Georgia offers up to 30% in transferable tax credits on film and TV production costs, plus an additional 10% increase on the base tax credit if the project includes a Georgia promotional logo."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-4","text":"Even as California lost a huge volume of production to other locations, there was still plenty of film production taking place in Los Angeles before this year. We were kept afloat by \"peak TV\", the glut of content that was required by the explosion of streaming services."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-5","text":"But 2022 was the peak of peak TV. Back then platforms such as Netflix, Amazon and Apple TV hemorrhaged billions of dollars to generate content to attract new subscribers, resulting in 633 scripted series being released that year. As the streamers' emphasis changed from subscriber growth to profitability, prices for the services went up and the number of new shows went down to 481 released in 2023, with the number expected to dip into the 300s within a few years."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-6","text":"If productions in Southern California dip below a critical level for too long, the industry's essential talent will drift away along with enormous sums of revenue. Persuading studios to film here would become much more challenging if we couldn't offer a deep bench of local film workers, on-screen talent and local businesses that support the entertainment industry."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-7","text":"That's why the California Film Commission and its Los Angeles counterpart, Film LA, now should act, before it's too late. These agencies and other government bodies should dramatically improve incentives to keep our current shows and attract new productions to Los Angeles. Let's go on with the show…and make sure the show doesn't go on without us."}]},"questions":[{"n":26,"stem":"The Art Directors Guild's statement reveals ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"people's reduced interest in film."},{"key":"B","text":"film workers' nostalgia for the past."},{"key":"C","text":"the appeal of Southern California."},{"key":"D","text":"the gloomy situation of Hollywood."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"美国艺术指导协会的声明揭示了____","choicesZh":{"A":"人们对电影的兴趣下降","B":"电影从业者对过去的怀念","C":"南加州的吸引力","D":"好莱坞的严峻形势"},"analysis":{"type":"Q26 用 reveals 配上专有机构名，问的是该声明所揭示的内容，本质上是问声明这件事在文中对应的事实，答案应能在原文找到原句支撑。","parse":"主语 The Art Directors Guild's statement 是名词性短语，谓语 reveals 后接宾语空位等待选项补全，是「主＋谓＋宾」的待补全陈述句，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"This is a reaction to Hollywood's decline, which is reaching a critical point for the industry and Southern California.","locateZh":"这是对好莱坞衰落的一种反应，这种衰落对该行业和南加州来说已达到临界点。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"people's reduced interest in film 把 Guild 声明所反映的「行业供给端衰落」偷换成「观众需求端兴趣下降」，原文未谈观众兴趣，谈的是制作端流失。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"film workers' nostalgia for the past 中的 nostalgia for the past（怀旧）在 P2 中完全没有依据；Guild 谈的是当下行业现状，并未追忆过去。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"the appeal of Southern California 暗示南加州具有吸引力，而 reaching a critical point for the industry and Southern California 恰恰说南加州正在失去吸引力、走向危机，二者方向相反。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判要找的是「好莱坞处于低迷／衰落」，the gloomy situation of Hollywood 直接对应 Hollywood's decline，且未引入文中没有的细节，是与预判同义的唯一选项。","pairs":[{"orig":"Hollywood's decline, which is reaching a critical point","opt":"the gloomy situation of Hollywood"},{"orig":"we cannot in good conscience encourage you to pursue our profession","opt":"the gloomy situation of Hollywood"}]}}}}},{"n":27,"stem":"The example of Georgia is used to illustrate the efforts to ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"lure production with tax incentives."},{"key":"B","text":"drive improvements in film facilities."},{"key":"C","text":"stimulate competition among states."},{"key":"D","text":"collect funds for film and TV making."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"Georgia的例子被用来说明____方面的努力","choicesZh":{"A":"用税收优惠吸引制作","B":"推动电影设施的改善","C":"刺激各州之间的竞争","D":"为影视制作筹集资金"},"analysis":{"type":"The example of Georgia is used to illustrate 是典型例证目的题句式，问的不是例子本身的细节而是它为某个论点服务的目的。","parse":"主干是 The example of Georgia is used to illustrate the efforts to ____，is used to illustrate 是被动＋不定式表用途，宾语 the efforts 后再接 to 不定式作定语补全，选项填的是不定式短语，结构清晰无陷阱。","locateEn":"Other regions in the United States, Canada and Europe have steadily increased incentives to attract TV shows and movies, leaving California in the dust. Georgia offers up to 30% in transferable tax credits on film and TV production costs, plus an additional 10% increase on the base tax credit if the project includes a Georgia promotional logo.","locateZh":"美国、加拿大和欧洲的其他地区一直在稳步加大激励力度，以吸引电视剧和电影制作，把加州甩在身后。佐治亚州对影视制作成本提供高达 30% 的可转让税收抵免，如果项目中包含佐治亚州的宣传标志，还可在基础税收抵免上再加 10%。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"lure production with tax incentives 同时覆盖了预判中的「吸引制作」和「税收激励」两个核心语义，是与论点句＋例证句联合表达完全一致的唯一选项。","pairs":[{"orig":"increased incentives to attract TV shows and movies","opt":"lure production with tax incentives"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"drive improvements in film facilities 把激励政策的目的偷换为改善影视设施，P3 通篇谈的是税收抵免吸引制作，对设施改造只字未提。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"stimulate competition among states 谈的是「激发州际竞争」这一抽象结果，而 Q27 问的是这个例子用来说明什么具体「efforts」；竞争是宏观推断，不是 Georgia 例子的直接目的。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"collect funds for film and TV making 把「税收抵免（给出优惠）」偷换为「为影视筹钱（吸纳资金）」，方向恰好相反——原文是政府给片方让利，而非政府向片方筹款。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":28,"stem":"Peak TV passed its peak as ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"streamers lost their technical advantages."},{"key":"B","text":"streamers changed their strategic priorities."},{"key":"C","text":"subscribers grew wary of large platforms."},{"key":"D","text":"subscribers were unhappy with new shows."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"巅峰电视时代度过了巅峰是因为____","choicesZh":{"A":"流媒体平台失去了技术优势","B":"流媒体平台改变了战略重点","C":"订阅者对大型平台产生了警惕","D":"订阅者对新节目不满意"},"analysis":{"type":"Q28 问的是巅峰已过这一现象发生的具体原因，原文应有直接表述，不需要跨句推断。","parse":"Peak TV passed its peak as ____ 中前半句是主句，as 引导原因状语从句，选项补的是从句主谓宾。Peak 既作专名一部分又作普通名词重复出现，属修辞而非陷阱。","locateEn":"As the streamers' emphasis changed from subscriber growth to profitability, prices for the services went up and the number of new shows went down to 481 released in 2023, with the number expected to dip into the 300s within a few years.","locateZh":"随着流媒体的重心从用户增长转向盈利能力，服务价格上涨，新剧数量下降至 2023 年的 481 部，预计未来几年将进一步跌入 300 多部区间。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"streamers lost their technical advantages 中 technical advantages 在 P5 乃至全段都没有出现；原文谈的是战略与财务问题，没有谈技术。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"streamers changed their strategic priorities 把原文的具体战略转变上升为「战略重心改变」这一上位概括，与预判完全吻合，是唯一直接对应原因句的选项。","pairs":[{"orig":"the streamers' emphasis changed from subscriber growth to profitability","opt":"streamers changed their strategic priorities"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"subscribers grew wary of large platforms 把「流媒体主动调整策略」张冠李戴为「订户对大平台心生戒备」，主体从 streamers 偷换成 subscribers；原文中 subscriber 只出现在 subscriber growth 这一战略对象上，未谈订户态度。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"subscribers were unhappy with new shows 把原因偷换为订户对新剧不满，原文只说新剧数量下降，未提订户对剧集质量的态度。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":29,"stem":"According to Paragraph 6, California's entertainment industry might face ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"a decline in product quality."},{"key":"B","text":"a demand for foreign talent."},{"key":"C","text":"a brain drain to other places."},{"key":"D","text":"a dramatic rise in labor costs."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据第6段,加州的娱乐产业可能面临____","choicesZh":{"A":"产品质量的下降","B":"对外国人才的需求","C":"人才流失到其他地方","D":"劳动力成本的急剧上升"},"analysis":{"type":"题干已锁定第 6 段并问产业可能面临什么，本质是看该段写了什么风险，无需推断。","parse":"According to Paragraph 6, California's entertainment industry might face ____ 中 According to Paragraph 6 是状语，主语是产业，might face 是谓语，选项填名词性宾语，结构简洁无陷阱。","locateEn":"If productions in Southern California dip below a critical level for too long, the industry's essential talent will drift away along with enormous sums of revenue.","locateZh":"如果南加州的影视制作量长期低于临界水平，行业的核心人才将与巨额收入一起流失。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"a decline in product quality 把后果偷换为产品质量下降，P6 谈的是人才与收入流失，并未提到剧集质量。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"a demand for foreign talent 暗示要从国外引进人才，与 the industry's essential talent will drift away 「本地人才流失」方向相反；原文担心的是留不住人，不是引进外人。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"a brain drain to other places 是预判「核心人才流失到别处」的标准术语化表达，直接对应 essential talent will drift away，无附加成分。","pairs":[{"orig":"the industry's essential talent will drift away","opt":"a brain drain to other places"},{"orig":"Persuading studios to film here would become much more challenging if we couldn't offer a deep bench of local film workers, on-screen talent and local businesses","opt":"a brain drain to other places"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"a dramatic rise in labor costs 把后果偷换为人力成本暴涨，P6 没有涉及劳动力价格变化，只谈人才与收入流失。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":30,"stem":"The author concludes the text by emphasizing that California should strive to ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"maintain its position in the industry."},{"key":"B","text":"attract more investment than it had."},{"key":"C","text":"pursue a higher standard of production."},{"key":"D","text":"strengthen coordination with other states."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"作者在文末强调加州应该努力____","choicesZh":{"A":"保持其在行业中的地位","B":"吸引比以往更多的投资","C":"追求更高的制作标准","D":"加强与其他州的协调"},"analysis":{"type":"Q30 问作者结尾强调的内容，本质是问全文中心主张／结论，需要串末段及全文行文走向。","parse":"The author concludes the text by emphasizing that California should strive to ____：主句 The author concludes the text，by 短语作方式状语，that 引导宾语从句 California should strive to ____，选项补 strive to 之后的不定式短语，结构清晰无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"maintain its position in the industry 直接对应末段号召，且能覆盖前文「别处崛起、peak TV 已过、人才将流失」共同指向的「再不行动就会失去地位」这一主线，是唯一与全文主旨一致的选项。","pairs":[{"orig":"make sure the show doesn't go on without us","opt":"maintain its position in the industry"},{"orig":"dramatically improve incentives to keep our current shows and attract new productions to Los Angeles","opt":"maintain its position in the industry"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"attract more investment than it had 把目的偷换为「比过去吸引更多投资」，原文呼吁的是改进激励留住剧目、吸引制作，并未把「投资」作为目标，更未与过去做比较；than it had 这一比较成分原文也无对应。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"pursue a higher standard of production 中 higher standard 在全文没有依据；原文谈的是数量、收入、人才流失，从未涉及制作标准／质量层面。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"strengthen coordination with other states 提议与别州加强协调，而原文 Other regions … leaving California in the dust 把别州明确定为竞争对手；末段呼吁的是与别州争夺制作，而非与之协调。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-3","kind":"reading","title":"Text 3","textNo":3,"directions":"Directions: Read the following four texts. Answer the questions after each text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET . (40 points)","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 3","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-1","text":"The pioneers of wireless saw it as a gift to all the people. Sir John Reith said that it would end \"isolation of the spirit\" and rejoiced: \"It does not matter how many thousands may be listening, there is always enough for others…the genius and the fool, the wealthy and the poor listen simultaneously.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-2","text":"Between two great wars this technological innovation built a new kind of national consciousness. Opening this week, a book and exhibition curated by Beatty Rubens at the Bodleian in Oxford records how radio changed everyday life from 1922 to 1939. She draws on letters, diaries and fiction, and a 1939 field notebook of verbatim audience research by Winifred Gill."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-3","text":"There's fun in testimonies of people enjoying the sheer newness. A cartoon mocks a group failing to converse because they're all in headphones. People report that broadcast music made workmen whistle new tunes. A woman says there have been fewer street fights since the arrival of the wireless but also less stopping and \"talking on the brush handle\"."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-4","text":"By and large the wireless was welcome. I loved the man from the Thirties research who found that wireless suddenly offered \"a lot of variety…things I thought I'd never be interested in…ice hockey perhaps\". True: for more than 80 pre-digital years, linear speech broadcasting brought the gift of serendipity, random enlivening of a car journey or dull manual task. In my own book about radio I recorded how, on one drive: \"I caught up with the news, learnt some 17th-century history, and was startlingly educated by an unpretentious programme on the history of the stethoscope.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-5","text":"But radio's enriching serendipity is ebbing. With multiple networks and countless podcasts, a smartphone user selects what to hear and when. And while it is wonderful to take a walk with anything in your headphones, infinite choice encourages us to shrink into niche interests and sympathetic beliefs."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-6","text":"A hundred years on from Marconi and Reith, is the art of mere listening endangered? Some will say the audiobook boom revives it, though I suppose you can then worry about the decline of reading. But inventions shape all of us and it is worth noticing when techno-social habits do change, and asking whether to control them a bit or shield the youngest. Whatever we do, innovation will happen. Today we fret about the isolating culture of smartphone-staring and selfie-vanity, but already in 1939 there was that lady regretting how, when all her street got wirelesses, it lost the neighbourly habit of \"talking on the brush handle\". It's enough to make a person put down the smartphone and go out front with a yard broom."}]},"questions":[{"n":31,"stem":"What can be learnt about wireless from Sir John Reith?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It was accessible to everyone."},{"key":"B","text":"It improved interpersonal relations."},{"key":"C","text":"It was a miracle of human ingenuity."},{"key":"D","text":"It led to a new era of isolating culture."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"从 John Reith 爵士那里可以了解到无线电的什么情况?","choicesZh":{"A":"它对每个人都是可及的。","B":"它改善了人际关系。","C":"它是人类智慧的奇迹。","D":"它导致了一个孤立文化的新时代。"},"analysis":{"type":"What can be learnt about wireless from Sir John Reith? 没有 infer/imply/suggest 等推断信号，问的是从某人原话里能读到的客观信息，属典型细节题。","parse":"What can be learnt about wireless from Sir John Reith? 为一般疑问的特殊问句，主干是 What can be learnt，about wireless 限定话题，from Sir John Reith 限定信息来源。句式平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"Sir John Reith said that it would end \"isolation of the spirit\" and rejoiced: \"It does not matter how many thousands may be listening, there is always enough for others…the genius and the fool, the wealthy and the poor listen simultaneously.\"","locateZh":"约翰·里斯爵士说，无线电会结束\"精神上的孤立\"，并欣然表示：\"无论有多少千人在收听都没关系，永远有足够的份额留给其他人……天才与傻瓜、富人与穷人可以同时收听。\"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"预判结论\"无线电不分阶层、人人可享\"与 It was accessible to everyone 几乎逐字对应，A 即为正确项。","pairs":[{"orig":"the genius and the fool, the wealthy and the poor listen simultaneously","opt":"accessible to everyone"},{"orig":"there is always enough for others","opt":"accessible"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"improved interpersonal relations 在 P1 中没有依据，Reith 谈的是\"人人可听\"而非人与人之间关系的改善——这是后文 P3 才出现的话题。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"a miracle of human ingenuity 是对技术本身的礼赞，原文 Reith 强调的是\"人人可享\"，而非\"人类智慧的奇迹\"——评价对象不同。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Reith 谈的是无线电能 end \"isolation of the spirit\"——终结精神孤立；led to a new era of isolating culture 反说成造成孤立文化，与原文方向完全相反。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":32,"stem":"What is the theme of the exhibition at the Bodleian in Oxford?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The impact of radio on its early audience."},{"key":"B","text":"The role of radio in public music education."},{"key":"C","text":"The innovation process of radio technology."},{"key":"D","text":"The eminent pioneers in radio broadcasting."}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"牛津 Bodleian 图书馆展览的主题是什么?","choicesZh":{"A":"无线电对其早期听众的影响。","B":"无线电在公共音乐教育中的作用。","C":"无线电技术的创新过程。","D":"无线电广播的杰出先驱。"},"analysis":{"type":"What is the theme of the exhibition at the Bodleian in Oxford? 问的是该展览的明确主题，能在文中直接找到客观依据，不需作者意图推断。","parse":"What is the theme of the exhibition at the Bodleian in Oxford? 主干 What is the theme，of 短语层层修饰展览。结构清晰，无陷阱。","locateEn":"Opening this week, a book and exhibition curated by Beatty Rubens at the Bodleian in Oxford records how radio changed everyday life from 1922 to 1939.","locateZh":"本周开幕、由 Beatty Rubens 在牛津 Bodleian 图书馆策划的一本书及一场展览，记录了 1922 年至 1939 年间无线电如何改变了日常生活。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"把动词 changed 转换为名词 impact、把时间段 1922-1939 概括为 early，且对象 everyday life 对应 audience，预判到锁定 A 自然完成。","pairs":[{"orig":"how radio changed everyday life from 1922 to 1939","opt":"The impact of radio on its early audience"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"public music education 在 P2 没有任何依据；P3 虽提到广播让工人哼新曲，但那只是众多影响之一，并非展览主题。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"The innovation process of radio technology 谈无线电技术本身的创新过程，但 P2 明说 records how radio changed everyday life——是社会生活的变化，不是技术演进。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"eminent pioneers in radio broadcasting 把展览说成是关于著名先驱的展览，但 P2 的描述对象是 everyday life，先驱只在 P1 出现一次，并非展览主题。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":33,"stem":"It is indicated in Paragraph 4 that ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the research on radio used to be inadequate."},{"key":"B","text":"the charm of radio remains in the digital age."},{"key":"C","text":"radio listeners could make unexpected gains."},{"key":"D","text":"radio shows have changed little over the years."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"第4段表明____","choicesZh":{"A":"对无线电的研究过去是不充分的。","B":"无线电的魅力在数字时代依然存在。","C":"无线电听众可能获得意外收获。","D":"无线电节目多年来变化很小。"},"analysis":{"type":"It is indicated in Paragraph 4 that 中 indicated 偏中性，问的是该段已明示的内容，并非作者隐含态度，归为细节题更稳。","parse":"It is indicated in Paragraph 4 that ____ it 形式主语 + that 引导真正主语从句；从句被选项补完，无陷阱。","locateEn":"for more than 80 pre-digital years, linear speech broadcasting brought the gift of serendipity, random enlivening of a car journey or dull manual task.","locateZh":"在数字时代之前的八十多年里，线性的语音广播带来了\"意外发现\"的礼物——它能随机点亮一段乏味的开车旅程或无聊的体力劳动。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"the research on radio used to be inadequate 谈研究本身的不充分，但 P4 谈的是听众的体验，而非 radio research 的研究水准。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"the charm of radio remains in the digital age 谈数字时代广播的魅力仍存，但 P4 的时间状语是 80 pre-digital years——前数字时代，并未声称数字时代仍存魅力；P5 的转折反而暗示在数字时代下广播的偶遇之乐在消退。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判得到的\"意外收获\"与 radio listeners could make unexpected gains 几乎逐字对应，C 即正确答案。","pairs":[{"orig":"the gift of serendipity","opt":"unexpected gains"},{"orig":"things I thought I'd never be interested in…ice hockey perhaps","opt":"unexpected"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"radio shows have changed little over the years 谈节目本身变化不大，但 P4 谈的是听众收获的性质（意外性），并未对节目内容的演变作纵向比较。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":34,"stem":"The expression \"talking on the brush handle\" in Paragraphs 3 and 6 refers to the act of ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"making a loud noise."},{"key":"B","text":"having a casual chat."},{"key":"C","text":"starting a trivial quarrel."},{"key":"D","text":"humming a popular song."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义句意题","stemZh":"第3段和第6段中的表达\"talking on the brush handle\"指的是____的行为","choicesZh":{"A":"制造大声噪音。","B":"进行随意闲聊。","C":"开始琐碎争吵。","D":"哼唱流行歌曲。"},"analysis":{"type":"The expression…refers to the act of ____ 问短语指代的行为，属典型的词义/短语句意题。","parse":"The expression \"talking on the brush handle\" in Paragraphs 3 and 6 refers to the act of ____ 主语 The expression，介词短语限定段落，refers to 谓语；act of 后接选项动名词。结构平铺直叙。","locateEn":"A woman says there have been fewer street fights since the arrival of the wireless but also less stopping and \"talking on the brush handle\".","locateZh":"一位女士说，自从无线电出现以来，街头打架变少了，但也少了人们驻足在扫帚柄上闲聊的情景。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"making a loud noise 谈大声噪音，但 P3 把它与 fewer street fights 并列，brush handle 不可能也是负面行为，与并列方向不符。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"P3 的并列与 P6 的 neighbourly habit 共同把 brush handle 锁定为邻里闲聊，B 与预判完全一致。","pairs":[{"orig":"less stopping and \"talking on the brush handle\"","opt":"having a casual chat"},{"orig":"lost the neighbourly habit of \"talking on the brush handle\"","opt":"casual chat"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"starting a trivial quarrel 谈引发小争吵，仍是负面活动，与 brush handle 在并列结构里的正面属性不符。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"humming a popular song 谈哼唱流行歌，是 P3 前文 workmen whistle new tunes 的内容，与 brush handle 所处的并列结构无关。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":35,"stem":"In the last paragraph, the author intends to express the opinion that ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"technology should be aimed at benefiting humans."},{"key":"B","text":"actions should be taken to revive the art of listening."},{"key":"C","text":"adolescents should form healthy social media habits."},{"key":"D","text":"people should adopt a sensible attitude to innovations."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"在最后一段,作者意图表达的观点是____","choicesZh":{"A":"技术应该以造福人类为目标。","B":"应该采取行动来复兴倾听的艺术。","C":"青少年应该养成健康的社交媒体习惯。","D":"人们应该对创新采取明智的态度。"},"analysis":{"type":"the author intends to express the opinion that ____ 限定在末段并问意图主张，属局部主旨题，需对末段做串线归纳。","parse":"In the last paragraph, the author intends to express the opinion that ____ 介词短语作状语限定段落，主干 the author intends to express the opinion，that 从句被选项补完。无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"technology should be aimed at benefiting humans 谈技术应以人为本，但末段并未对技术开发的目标导向作任何规范性主张——作者只谈如何应对，不谈如何研发。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"actions should be taken to revive the art of listening 把恢复听的艺术作为主张，但末段 is the art of mere listening endangered? 只是抛出问题，作者随后用 Some will say…though I suppose you can then worry about… 的让步否定了\"复兴听之艺术\"作为主张的强度。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"adolescents should form healthy social media habits 把建议对象缩到青少年的社交媒体习惯，但原文 shield the youngest 只是建议中的一小部分，且谈的是\"保护\"而非\"养成习惯\"。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"末段建议被概括为\"理性看待创新\"，与 people should adopt a sensible attitude to innovations 一一对应，D 即正确答案。","pairs":[{"orig":"asking whether to control them a bit or shield the youngest","opt":"a sensible attitude to innovations"},{"orig":"innovation will happen","opt":"innovations"}]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-4","kind":"reading","title":"Text 4","textNo":4,"directions":"Directions: Read the following four texts. Answer the questions after each text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET . (40 points)","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 4","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-1","text":"When Tom Swetnam joined the U.S. Forest Service in the 1970s, his mandate was to \"put everything out,\" he recalled. But when Swetnam enrolled in graduate school at the University of Arizona's Laboratory of Tree-Ring Research, he was surprised to find a record of repeated blazes dating back hundreds of years before European colonists arrived on the continent. Some of the trees he analyzed bore more than 20 fire scars among their rings."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-2","text":"The fact that fires happened so often meant they couldn't have been severe enough to kill most trees. Instead, a growing body of research showed that frequent, low-severity fires made many ecosystems healthier. They rid the forest of dead and sick trees, reducing competition and curbing the spread of disease. Because flammable material couldn't build up on the landscape, blazes tended to move slowly and peter out when they reached the footprints of previous burns."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-3","text":"In 2022, Swetnam and other scientists teamed up to compile a database of fire-scarred trees from across the continent. Their North American tree-ring fire-scar network (NAFSN) provided the basis for a study published last month. In the study, the researchers compared the historical fire cadence with the wildfires recorded over the past few decades, and uncovered a striking shortfall. The NAFSN sites experienced less than a quarter of the number of fires that would have been expected without fire suppression."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-4","text":"This deficit is a testament to the effectiveness of modern firefighting, said Kelly Martin, a past president of the International Association of Wildland Fire. \"Yet the combined consequences of suppression and climate change have eroded humanity's ability to suppress fires, particularly those that ignite under the most dangerous weather conditions.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-5","text":"To prevent entire ecosystems from going up in smoke, Martin said, people must bring healthy fire back to places that need it. At Yosemite National Park, Martin oversaw the use of what is known as prescribed burns to make the landscape more resilient. These fires were carefully planned and intentionally ignited during periods when weather kept the blazes easy to control, and helped eliminate some of the fuel that had built up around the important park's facilities. Research shows that these prescribed burns make subsequent wildfires less severe, even if later fires happen under the most dangerous weather conditions."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-6","text":"Yet even as scientists and public officials increasingly agree on the need for more fires in our forests, climate change is making this tactic more challenging, experts said. \"It's a double-edged sword because wildfires are getting more severe and larger under climate change and we need this work even more, but then the work gets more challenging,\" said Susan Prichard, a fire ecologist at the University of Washington."}]},"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"According to Paragraph 1, Swetnam was surprised by ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the scarcity of tree-ring research in the U.S."},{"key":"B","text":"the firefighting measures in ancient Europe."},{"key":"C","text":"the forest management practices in the 1970s."},{"key":"D","text":"the number of wildfires in precolonial times."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据第1段,Swetnam对什么感到惊讶","choicesZh":{"A":"美国树木年轮研究的稀缺性","B":"古代欧洲的灭火措施","C":"20世纪70年代的森林管理做法","D":"前殖民时代野火的数量"},"analysis":{"type":"According to Paragraph 1, Swetnam was surprised by 直问段内一个具体事实，无 infer/imply/suggest 等推断信号。","parse":"Swetnam was surprised by ____ 是含被动语态的不完整陈述句，by 后接的就是让他惊讶的对象，选项是这个 by 短语的延续；句法上无陷阱。","locateEn":"But when Swetnam enrolled in graduate school at the University of Arizona's Laboratory of Tree-Ring Research, he was surprised to find a record of repeated blazes dating back hundreds of years before European colonists arrived on the continent.","locateZh":"但是当 Swetnam 进入亚利桑那大学树轮研究室读研时，他惊讶地发现一份记录显示，早在欧洲殖民者抵达这片大陆几百年前就已存在反复发生的火灾。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"the scarcity of tree-ring research in the U.S. 说“美国树轮研究稀缺“，但 P1 只提到 Swetnam 进入了一所树轮研究室，未评价研究是否稀缺。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"the firefighting measures in ancient Europe. 说“古代欧洲的灭火措施“，但 European colonists 只用欧洲殖民者作时间参照，从未谈古代欧洲如何灭火。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"the forest management practices in the 1970s. 看似贴近 When Tom Swetnam joined the U.S. Forest Service in the 1970s, his mandate was to put everything out，但这是他的职责，不是让他惊讶的内容；让他惊讶的在 But 之后。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判的“殖民前反复火灾“两要素——殖民前 + 火灾很多——恰好被 the number of wildfires in precolonial times 一句覆盖，无多无少。","pairs":[{"orig":"repeated blazes dating back hundreds of years before European colonists arrived on the continent","opt":"the number of wildfires in precolonial times"},{"orig":"Some of the trees he analyzed bore more than 20 fire scars among their rings.","opt":"the number of wildfires in precolonial times"}]}}}}},{"n":37,"stem":"Paragraph 2 mainly focuses on ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the causes of previous burns."},{"key":"B","text":"the treatment of diseased trees."},{"key":"C","text":"the benefits of low-severity fires."},{"key":"D","text":"the importance of forest ecosystems."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"第2段主要关注","choicesZh":{"A":"以往火灾的原因","B":"病树的处理","C":"低烈度火灾的益处","D":"森林生态系统的重要性"},"analysis":{"type":"Paragraph 2 mainly focuses on 问的是整段焦点，是典型的段落主旨题（属主旨题的段级版本）。","parse":"Paragraph 2 mainly focuses on ____ 是简单陈述句加空格，on 的宾语就是焦点对象，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"the causes of previous burns. 表“旧火的成因”，但 previous burns 只在末句作位置参照（火走到旧火痕处就熄），并未追溯其原因。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"the treatment of diseased trees. 来自 They rid the forest of dead and sick trees，但这只是益处之一，远不能覆盖段内的多项并列益处。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判的“低强度火带来好处”被 the benefits of low-severity fires. 直接命中，无任何遗漏。","pairs":[{"orig":"frequent, low-severity fires made many ecosystems healthier","opt":"the benefits of low-severity fires."},{"orig":"reducing competition and curbing the spread of disease","opt":"the benefits of low-severity fires."}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"the importance of forest ecosystems. 说“森林生态系统的重要性”，但本段焦点是“低强度火带来的好处”，森林本身的重要性并不是段落话题。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":38,"stem":"What did the study find about the wildfires over the past few decades?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Their intensity has vastly fluctuated."},{"key":"B","text":"Their frequency has markedly decreased."},{"key":"C","text":"Their threats have been underestimated."},{"key":"D","text":"Their records have been misinterpreted."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"关于过去几十年的野火,该研究发现了什么","choicesZh":{"A":"它们的强度大幅波动","B":"它们的频率显著下降","C":"它们的威胁被低估了","D":"它们的记录被误读了"},"analysis":{"type":"What did the study find about the wildfires over the past few decades? 直接问研究的发现，是被研究句承载的客观事实，归类为细节题。","parse":"What did the study find about the wildfires over the past few decades? 是 wh- 一般疑问句；about 短语限定 find 的范围；选项均为 Their + 名词 + 谓语 句式，是对 wildfires 这一主题的属性描述，结构清晰。","locateEn":"In the study, the researchers compared the historical fire cadence with the wildfires recorded over the past few decades, and uncovered a striking shortfall. The NAFSN sites experienced less than a quarter of the number of fires that would have been expected without fire suppression.","locateZh":"在这项研究中，研究者把历史上的火灾节律与近几十年记录的野火相比较，发现了一个惊人的缺口：NAFSN 各站点经历的火灾数量不到原本（在没有灭火措施时）应当出现数量的四分之一。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Their intensity has vastly fluctuated. 谈强度的剧烈波动，但 P3 只比较“数量“，未对强度做时间序列上的波动评估。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判的“实际数量大幅下降“被 Their frequency has markedly decreased. 同义替换覆盖，且没有引入定位句以外的信息。","pairs":[{"orig":"less than a quarter of the number of fires that would have been expected","opt":"Their frequency has markedly decreased."},{"orig":"uncovered a striking shortfall","opt":"Their frequency has markedly decreased."}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Their threats have been underestimated. 说野火的威胁被低估，但 P3 全段未对野火威胁的“被认知程度“做评论。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"Their records have been misinterpreted. 说野火记录被误解，但 P3 没说任何“既有解读出错“的内容，研究只是做了新比较。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":39,"stem":"What can be inferred about modern firefighting?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Its workforce needs more training."},{"key":"B","text":"Its effectiveness is questioned by the public."},{"key":"C","text":"It may render traditional tactics useless."},{"key":"D","text":"It may make severe fires harder to put out."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"推断题","stemZh":"关于现代消防可以推断出什么","choicesZh":{"A":"其工作人员需要更多培训","B":"其有效性受到公众质疑","C":"它可能使传统战术失效","D":"它可能使严重火灾更难扑灭"},"analysis":{"type":"What can be inferred about modern firefighting? 句中 inferred 是推断题硬标志。","parse":"What can be inferred about modern firefighting? 是含情态被动 can be inferred 的 wh- 疑问句，about 短语界定推断对象；选项主语是 Its，回指 modern firefighting；无陷阱。","locateEn":"Yet the combined consequences of suppression and climate change have eroded humanity's ability to suppress fires, particularly those that ignite under the most dangerous weather conditions.","locateZh":"然而，灭火措施与气候变化的共同后果已经削弱了人类压制火灾的能力——尤其是那些在最危险天气条件下被点燃的火。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Its workforce needs more training. 把问题归结到消防员培训，但 P4 没有任何句子提到培训、人手或队伍。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"Its effectiveness is questioned by the public. 说公众质疑现代灭火，但 This deficit is a testament to the effectiveness of modern firefighting 恰恰肯定了它的有效性，文中也没有公众态度。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"It may render traditional tactics useless. 说现代灭火让传统手段变得无用，但 P4 既未对比新旧手段，也未否定任何传统做法。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判的“极端条件下的火更难扑灭“恰好是 It may make severe fires harder to put out. 的客观转述，且 may 一词正对应推断的语气。","pairs":[{"orig":"eroded humanity's ability to suppress fires","opt":"It may make severe fires harder to put out."},{"orig":"those that ignite under the most dangerous weather conditions","opt":"It may make severe fires harder to put out."}]}}}}},{"n":40,"stem":"Both Martin and Prichard would agree that ____","options":[{"key":"A","text":"it is challenging to predict large wildfires."},{"key":"B","text":"it is urgent to assess the use of planned fires."},{"key":"C","text":"it is necessary to introduce prescribed burns."},{"key":"D","text":"it is rewarding to double fire detection efforts."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"态度题","stemZh":"Martin和Prichard都会同意","choicesZh":{"A":"预测大型野火具有挑战性","B":"评估计划火灾的使用是紧迫的","C":"引入规定烧除是必要的","D":"加倍火灾探测工作是值得的"},"analysis":{"type":"Both Martin and Prichard would agree that 问的是两人共同的态度/立场，是态度题的双人共识子类型。","parse":"Both Martin and Prichard would agree that ____ 是用 would agree 引出宾语从句的不完整陈述句；选项是 it is + 形容词 + to do 句式的延续；从句结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"it is challenging to predict large wildfires. 谈“预测大型野火困难“，但 Martin 与 Prichard 都未谈预测，wildfires are getting more severe and larger 只是给出趋势，而不是说预测难。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"it is urgent to assess the use of planned fires. 说“紧迫地评估计划火的使用“，但两位专家都没有谈评估这件事——他们直接谈“使用“。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"C 项是 Martin 的明确主张 + Prichard 的强烈呼应的最大公约数；预判的“共同支持引入受控火“正是 C 项的内容。","pairs":[{"orig":"Martin oversaw the use of what is known as prescribed burns to make the landscape more resilient","opt":"it is necessary to introduce prescribed burns."},{"orig":"we need this work even more","opt":"it is necessary to introduce prescribed burns."}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"it is rewarding to double fire detection efforts. 说“加倍火灾探测有回报“，但 P5、P6 都没提火灾探测/侦测，更没说要“加倍“。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-b","kind":"ordering","title":"Section II Part B 段落排序","directions":"Directions: The following paragraphs are given in a wrong order. For questions 41-45, you are required to reorganize these paragraphs into a coherent text by choosing from the list A-H and filling them into the numbered boxes. Paragraphs F, H, and C have been correctly placed. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET . (10 points)","points":10,"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-b-1","label":"A","text":"And just read a single poem. In his Oxford lectures, Seamus Heaney argued that a poem draws a picture of reality, a \"glimpsed alternative\" that sets up a contradiction with your own, in ways little and large. The negotiation, between you and it, is the heart of the matter. What does the poet see that you don't? What does the difference mean? It could be one of the best conversations you ever have. Forget self-help books; reading is self-help in action."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-2","label":"B","text":"But for the most part, this isn't what the business community does. I have yet to meet a chief executive who reads regularly. Many skip newspapers, and magazines are a stretch. They don't have time, they say. It's inefficient; they can get the information they need from those around them. At a pinch, they might pick up a business book before a long flight, in the hope that, like a cookbook, it will provide a foolproof recipe. Some are drawn to what I think of as \"business car crash\" books — the stories of Theranos, Purdue, or WeWork. But outside those narrow pools of interest, a vast ocean awaits, bountiful with simmering ideas, mental adventure and imaginative refreshment."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-3","label":"C","text":"Neuroscientists have been at pains to demonstrate that the pleasure a book provides isn't indulgence; it's good for you. Reading will keep you better informed about the world but it can also improve our tech-shattered ability to concentrate. Standing in the shoes of others fine tunes our social understanding, useful as we struggle to understand friends, neighbours, customers and co-workers. Different parts of our brain engage as we simulate scenes, characters and mental states. Our imagination — remember that? — is rekindled."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-4","label":"D","text":"It is undoubtedly true that all work and no play really does make Jack, or Jill, dull. The cure is right at hand. Reading is cheap, easy and, most important, it's fun. Liberate your imagination this year."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-5","label":"E","text":"We are living through a golden age of science writing, so lucid and accessible that even lay readers can relish the unpredictability of discovery. Daunted by uncertainty? Stand in the shoes of scientists and witness the degree to which breakthroughs emerge from accidents, conflict and sheer mental stamina. \"We are never sure of anything,\" says the physicist (and writer) Carlo Rovelli."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-6","label":"F","text":"You don't need to get out more. If, like most business people, you spend your life dashing from office to plane, train to home, boardroom to washroom, what you really need to do is stay in more, sit down — and read a book."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-7","label":"G","text":"Reading has also been found to make us more helpful, to reduce bias, and even to increase longevity — something we will enjoy all the more if we have a good book in our hands. (And yes, all these benefits are more closely associated with physical books than digital ones.)"},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-8","label":"H","text":"Read fiction. Any fiction. Free yourself from algorithms and choose—anything. You don't need technology for an immersive experience—just surrender to narratives across time and place. Modern (Sebastian Barry or Olga Tokarczuk), classic (Virginia Woolf or James Baldwin) or genre (Stephen King, Margaret Atwood, Georges Simenon)—it doesn't matter. Fiction invites you to loiter unseen in the lives of others. We are living through a golden age of translation too, so you can go anywhere in time or place."}],"choices":[{"key":"A","text":"And just read a single poem. In his Oxford lectures, Seamus Heaney argued that a poem draws a picture of reality, a \"glimpsed alternative\" that sets up a contradiction with your own, in ways little and large. The negotiation, between you and it, is the heart of the matter. What does the poet see that you don't? What does the difference mean? It could be one of the best conversations you ever have. Forget self-help books; reading is self-help in action."},{"key":"B","text":"But for the most part, this isn't what the business community does. I have yet to meet a chief executive who reads regularly. Many skip newspapers, and magazines are a stretch. They don't have time, they say. It's inefficient; they can get the information they need from those around them. At a pinch, they might pick up a business book before a long flight, in the hope that, like a cookbook, it will provide a foolproof recipe. Some are drawn to what I think of as \"business car crash\" books — the stories of Theranos, Purdue, or WeWork. But outside those narrow pools of interest, a vast ocean awaits, bountiful with simmering ideas, mental adventure and imaginative refreshment."},{"key":"C","text":"Neuroscientists have been at pains to demonstrate that the pleasure a book provides isn't indulgence; it's good for you. Reading will keep you better informed about the world but it can also improve our tech-shattered ability to concentrate. Standing in the shoes of others fine tunes our social understanding, useful as we struggle to understand friends, neighbours, customers and co-workers. Different parts of our brain engage as we simulate scenes, characters and mental states. Our imagination — remember that? — is rekindled."},{"key":"D","text":"It is undoubtedly true that all work and no play really does make Jack, or Jill, dull. The cure is right at hand. Reading is cheap, easy and, most important, it's fun. Liberate your imagination this year."},{"key":"E","text":"We are living through a golden age of science writing, so lucid and accessible that even lay readers can relish the unpredictability of discovery. Daunted by uncertainty? Stand in the shoes of scientists and witness the degree to which breakthroughs emerge from accidents, conflict and sheer mental stamina. \"We are never sure of anything,\" says the physicist (and writer) Carlo Rovelli."},{"key":"F","text":"You don't need to get out more. If, like most business people, you spend your life dashing from office to plane, train to home, boardroom to washroom, what you really need to do is stay in more, sit down — and read a book."},{"key":"G","text":"Reading has also been found to make us more helpful, to reduce bias, and even to increase longevity — something we will enjoy all the more if we have a good book in our hands. (And yes, all these benefits are more closely associated with physical books than digital ones.)"},{"key":"H","text":"Read fiction. Any fiction. Free yourself from algorithms and choose—anything. You don't need technology for an immersive experience—just surrender to narratives across time and place. Modern (Sebastian Barry or Olga Tokarczuk), classic (Virginia Woolf or James Baldwin) or genre (Stephen King, Margaret Atwood, Georges Simenon)—it doesn't matter. Fiction invites you to loiter unseen in the lives of others. We are living through a golden age of translation too, so you can go anywhere in time or place."}],"questions":[{"n":41,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"B"},{"n":42,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"E"},{"n":43,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"A"},{"n":44,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"G"},{"n":45,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"D"}],"order":["F","B","E","H","A","C","G","D"],"fixed":{"0":"F","3":"H","5":"C"},"chain":"F → 41. → 42. → H → 43. → C → 44. → 45.","orderChain":"正确顺序 预给 F → 41 B → 42 E → 预给 H → 43 A → 预给 C → 44 G → 45 D","seams":["【承接前一段】 段 F 指代衔接：B 段首的 this 正回指 F 段提出的『该做的事——坐下读书』，But 转折说商界人大多不这么做 。F 段尾立起主张 what you really need to do is ... read a book，B 段首 But for the most part, this isn't what the business community does 的 this 消解到这个主张：商界人本该读书，但大多不读。But 的转折恰好把『主张』与『现状』咬合在 F 之后，41 紧贴 given 段 F，位置最好定。","【承接前一段】 段 B 语义承接：B 段尾把书海比作『等你的汪洋宝藏』作总说，E 紧接着兑现第一项——推荐科普读物 。B 段尾 a vast ocean awaits, bountiful with simmering ideas 抛出『书海宝藏』的总览，E 段首 golden age of science writing 是这总说后的第一项具体推荐（科普）。无显式回指词，靠『总说→分项展开』的内容逻辑顺承（弱锚）。","【引出后一段】 段 H 词汇复现：E 与 H 共用句式 We are living through a golden age of...，H 段尾的 too 把『翻译黄金时代』叠在 E 的『科普黄金时代』之上 。E 段首 We are living through a golden age of science writing 与 H 段尾 We are living through a golden age of translation too 句式复现、且 H 用 too 呼应，说明 H 紧接 E 之后（E 在 42、H 是 given 紧随其后）。这条词汇复现把 42=E 与 given 段 H 钉在一起，是本位最硬的证据。","【承接前一段】 段 H 逻辑连接词：A 段首 And + read a single poem 与 H 的 Read fiction 同为『读XX』祈使句并列，And 表又添一项 。H（given）以 Read fiction. Any fiction 推荐小说，A 段首 And just read a single poem 用 And 续上同款祈使推荐（读诗），句式与逻辑都紧贴 H。43 紧跟 given 段 H，靠这条并列链即可锁定。","【引出后一段】 段 C 语义承接：A 段尾把读书定性为『行动中的自助』，C（given）紧接着用脑科学论证读书『对你有好处』，话题顺承 。A 段尾 reading is self-help in action 把落点从『读什么』转到『读书的价值』，C 段首 it's good for you 正好接着展开益处。无显式回指词，是靠『价值—益处』的内容逻辑顺承（弱锚），但与前邻 H 的强并列链互为印证，43=A 站得稳。","【承接前一段】 段 C 逻辑连接词：G 段首 also 表递进，在 C 已列的益处之上『再加一批』益处，紧跟 C 。C（given）已列读书益处 it's good for you 并细数增见闻、提专注等，G 段首 Reading has also been found 的 also 表明是在 C 的基础上追加（助人、减偏见、延寿）。44 紧贴 given 段 C，靠 also 的递进链锁定。","【承接前一段】 段 G 语义承接：G 把益处列尽（all these benefits），D 紧接着收尾：一味工作令人乏味、读书是解药 。G 段尾 all these benefits 标志益处论证收口，D 段首 The cure 回指由此立起的『读书少之弊』并给出结论。两段是『论证完毕→收尾升华』的内容顺承，D 段尾 Liberate your imagination this year 作全文落幕，45 是尾段位置。"]},{"id":"section2-part-c","kind":"translation","title":"Section II Part C 英译汉","directions":"Directions: Read the following text carefully and then translate the underlined segments into Chinese. Write your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. ( 10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-c-1","text":"Science education today revolves around the idea of scientific literacy — the base-level knowledge about science that nonscientists require to effectively get on in the world. This concept has served as a central goal for curriculum developers, local school boards, business and community leaders, and policymakers ever since its introduction nearly 80 years ago."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-2","text":"(46) Tracing the history of the term, we can see how the definition of scientific literacy has shifted over time, muddying the waters when it comes to determining the goals of science education. And that's a shame, because there is much to recommend in the idea of scientific literacy as it was originally articulated in 1945, a time when science appeared to be the key to progress and scientists seemingly held the fate of the world in their hands. (47) A return to that version of scientific literacy, which focused more on teaching what science is and how it works and less on memorizing scientific facts, seems like something society today desperately needs."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-3","text":"In the United States, the desire to provide the public with a general, nontechnical education in science originated as far back as the late 1800s. (48) Educators advanced the idea of having students complete detailed laboratory exercises in high schools in the belief that such work was beneficial primarily as a way to enhance logical reasoning and observational skills. The development in 1915 of the popular new subject \"general science\" was another effort to train students to apply the principles of science to everyday, nonscience problems."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-4","text":"Although these efforts were aimed at the nonscience-bound student, they never really made their way into mainstream thought and public discourse as a means to rally widespread support for the importance of science teaching in schools. (49) It wasn't until the phrase \"scientific literacy\" came along in the 1940s that science had the formidable slogan it needed to command public attention and make improving science education an important national goal."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-5","text":"(50) The intense focus on scientific literacy in the United States originally grew out of the critical role of science and technology during World War II, as well as the perceived deficiencies of American soldiers. As the war unfolded, science rapidly assumed a central role. Battles increasingly depended on new military technologies such as radar and the proximity fuze. Science-based analytical approaches proved remarkably successful in the hunt for German submarines in the Atlantic Ocean. And there was the (then-secret) work building the world's first atomic bomb. As a result, scientists — physicists in particular — found themselves in high demand."}]},"sentences":[{"n":46,"en":"Tracing the history of the term, we can see how the definition of scientific literacy has shifted over time, muddying the waters when it comes to determining the goals of science education.","refZh":"追溯这一术语的历史，我们可以看到“科学素养”的定义是如何随时间不断变化的，而这就使得在确定科学教育的目标时变得愈发模糊不清。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"Tracing the history of the term,","lit":"追溯这一术语的历史，","role":"（现在分词短语作时间状语）","note":"难点： 分词状语 + the term 指代 Tracing 是现在分词作状语，逻辑主语是后面的 we，译成“当我们追溯……时”或直接“追溯……”；the term 回指 scientific literacy，补成“这一术语（科学素养）”。"},{"en":"we can see","lit":"我们可以看到","role":"（主句主谓）","note":""},{"en":"how the definition of scientific literacy has shifted over time,","lit":"“科学素养”的定义是如何随时间不断变化的，","role":"（how 引导的宾语从句）","note":"难点： shifted over time shift 此处取“（不断）变化、改变”，over time = 随着时间推移；how 从句作 see 的宾语，译“……是如何……的”。"},{"en":"muddying the waters when it comes to determining the goals of science education.","lit":"（这）把水搅浑，在确定科学教育的目标时（造成困扰）。","role":"（现在分词作结果状语 + when 从句）","note":"难点： muddy the waters 熟语 + when it comes to muddy the waters 是固定习语，字面“搅浑水”，实指“把问题搞得混乱不清”，不能直译；when it comes to doing = 一涉及/在……方面，to 是介词后接动名词 determining。"}]},{"n":47,"en":"A return to that version of scientific literacy, which focused more on teaching what science is and how it works and less on memorizing scientific facts, seems like something society today desperately needs.","refZh":"回归科学素养的那个（1945 年的）版本——它更注重教授科学是什么、如何运作，而不那么强调背诵科学事实——似乎正是当今社会迫切需要的东西。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"A return to that version of scientific literacy,","lit":"回到那个版本的科学素养，","role":"（主语）","note":"难点： that version 指代 + 名词化 return that version 回指 as it was originally articulated in 1945，即“1945 年最初的那个版本”；return 是抽象名词，译时可化为动词“回归 / 回到”。"},{"en":"which focused more on teaching what science is and how it works and less on memorizing scientific facts,","lit":"（那个版本）更注重教“科学是什么、如何运作”，而不那么注重背诵科学事实，","role":"（which 引导的非限定性定语从句）","note":"难点： more on … and less on … 对比 focus more on A and less on B 是“多重 A、少重 B”的对比结构；what science is / how it works 是两个并列宾语从句，译“科学是什么、（科学）如何运作”。"},{"en":"seems like something society today desperately needs.","lit":"似乎正是当今社会迫切需要的东西。","role":"（谓语 + 省略 that 的定语从句）","note":"难点： something (that) society needs something 后省略了关系词 that，society today desperately needs 是修饰 something 的定语从句；desperately 程度词译“迫切地”。"}]},{"n":48,"en":"Educators advanced the idea of having students complete detailed laboratory exercises in high schools in the belief that such work was beneficial primarily as a way to enhance logical reasoning and observational skills.","refZh":"教育工作者提出，让高中生完成详尽的实验室练习；他们相信，这类练习之所以有益，主要是因为它是一种提升逻辑推理能力和观察能力的途径。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"Educators advanced the idea of having students complete detailed laboratory exercises in high schools","lit":"教育工作者提出了让高中生完成详尽实验室练习的想法","role":"（主干 + of 动名词同位内容）","note":"难点： advance the idea + have sb do advance 此处是“提出（主张/想法）”，非“前进”；have students complete 是“使役 have + 宾语 + 动词原形”，译“让学生完成”。"},{"en":"in the belief that such work was beneficial primarily","lit":"他们相信这种练习的益处主要在于","role":"（in the belief that 同位语从句作状语）","note":"难点： in the belief that + such work 指代 in the belief that… 是“怀着……的信念 / 因为相信……”；such work 回指前文 detailed laboratory exercises，译“这类（实验）练习”。"},{"en":"as a way to enhance logical reasoning and observational skills.","lit":"作为一种提升逻辑推理和观察能力的途径。","role":"（方式状语 + 不定式）","note":"难点： as a way to do as a way to enhance… = 作为提升……的一种方式；logical reasoning / observational skills 译“逻辑推理（能力）/ 观察能力”。"}]},{"n":49,"en":"It wasn't until the phrase \"scientific literacy\" came along in the 1940s that science had the formidable slogan it needed to command public attention and make improving science education an important national goal.","refZh":"直到 20 世纪 40 年代“科学素养”这一说法问世，科学才拥有了它所需要的那句强有力的口号——用以赢得公众的关注，并使改善科学教育成为一项重要的国家目标。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"It wasn't until the phrase \"scientific literacy\" came along in the 1940s","lit":"直到 20 世纪 40 年代“科学素养”这一说法出现，","role":"（not until 强调句的状语部分）","note":"难点： It was not until … that … 强调句 这是 It was not until A that B 的强调句型，固定译“直到 A，才 B”，不要按字面译“它不是直到”；come along 取“出现、问世”。"},{"en":"that science had the formidable slogan it needed","lit":"科学才有了它所需要的那句强有力的口号，","role":"（强调句主句 + 省略 that 的定语从句）","note":"难点： slogan (that) it needed the slogan 后省略关系词 that，it needed 是定语从句“它所需要的”；formidable 取“强大的、令人敬畏的”，修饰 slogan 译“强有力的”。"},{"en":"to command public attention and make improving science education an important national goal.","lit":"用以吸引公众注意，并把改善科学教育变成一项重要的国家目标。","role":"（不定式作定语（slogan 的用途））","note":"难点： make + 动名词宾语 + 宾补 command public attention = 赢得/吸引公众关注；make improving science education an important national goal 是 make + 宾语(improving…动名词) + 宾补(a goal)，译“使改善科学教育成为……目标”。"}]},{"n":50,"en":"The intense focus on scientific literacy in the United States originally grew out of the critical role of science and technology during World War II, as well as the perceived deficiencies of American soldiers.","refZh":"美国之所以高度关注科学素养，最初源于二战期间科学技术所发挥的关键作用，以及人们所认为的美国士兵在这方面的不足。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"The intense focus on scientific literacy in the United States","lit":"美国对科学素养的高度关注","role":"（主语）","note":"难点： intense focus 名词短语 intense focus on… = 对……的高度/强烈关注；in the United States 作定语，译“美国（国内）对……的关注”。"},{"en":"originally grew out of the critical role of science and technology during World War II,","lit":"最初源于二战期间科学技术所起的关键作用，","role":"（谓语（grow out of）+ 时间状语）","note":"难点： grow out of 短语动词 grow out of = 源于、产生于（不取字面“长出”）；the critical role of… during World War II 译“二战期间……的关键作用”。"},{"en":"as well as the perceived deficiencies of American soldiers.","lit":"以及人们所认为的美国士兵的不足。","role":"（as well as 并列成分）","note":"难点： perceived 熟词僻义 perceived 此处取“被认为的、（人们）感觉到的”，不是“感知”；deficiencies = 缺陷、不足；as well as 把它与前面的 role 并列，同作 grew out of 的宾语。"}]}]},{"id":"section3-part-a","kind":"writing","title":"Section III Part A 写作","directions":"51. Directions: Read the following email from your friend Paul and write him a reply. --- Hi Li Ming, I was really moved by the Chinese families' handwritten letters you posted yesterday. They are priceless! Could you please tell me a bit more about them? And are they currently on public display somewhere? I'm very keen to see them in person. Thanks. Yours, Paul --- You should write about 100 words on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not use your own name in the email; use \"Li Ming\" instead. (10 points)","prompt":"51. Directions: Read the following email from your friend Paul and write him a reply. --- Hi Li Ming, I was really moved by the Chinese families' handwritten letters you posted yesterday. They are priceless! Could you please tell me a bit more about them? And are they currently on public display somewhere? I'm very keen to see them in person. Thanks. Yours, Paul --- You should write about 100 words on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not use your own name in the email; use \"Li Ming\" instead. (10 points)","points":10,"image":null,"model":{"essay":"Dear Paul, I'm so glad you liked the handwritten letters, and I'd love to share more. These letters were written by ordinary Chinese families over the past century. Many were sent between parents and children living apart, recording daily worries, encouragement and love. They are priceless because they preserve real family memories and reflect how our society has changed. Yes, they are on public display. The letters are being exhibited at the City Library until the end of August, open daily from nine to five. If you like, I could go with you next weekend and show you around. Yours, Li Ming","translation":"亲爱的 Paul：\n很高兴你喜欢那些手写信件，我很乐意多讲讲。\n这些信件出自过去一个世纪里普通的中国家庭。许多是分隔两地的父母与子女之间的往来，记录着日常的牵挂、鼓励与爱。它们之所以珍贵，是因为保存了真实的家庭记忆，也映照出我们社会的变迁。\n是的，它们正在公开展出。这些信件在市图书馆展出，一直持续到八月底，每天上午九点到下午五点开放。如果你愿意，下个周末我可以陪你一起去，带你逛逛。\n此致 李明","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"开场破题","text":"【第 1 段】 开场破题 。开头 \"I'm so glad you liked the handwritten letters, and I'd love to share more.\" 一句称呼后即破题，落实了 guide1 的‘一句破题’句架，不寒暄拖沓。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"三连介绍","text":"【第 2 段】 三连介绍 。本段按来历（written by ordinary Chinese families）、内容（recording daily worries, encouragement and love）、价值（priceless because they preserve real family memories）三层展开，正是 guide2 的背景—内容—意义三连，回扣了 Paul 说的 They are priceless!。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"先答后补","text":"【第 3 段】 先答后补 。以 \"Yes, they are on public display.\" 正面回答第二问，再给展馆与开放时间，末句 \"I could go with you next weekend\" 发出邀约，完整落实 guide3。"},{"tag":"【第 4 段】","core":"守住署名","text":"【第 4 段】 守住署名 。以 Yours 收尾、落款 Li Ming，满足 use \"Li Ming\" instead，全程未出现真实姓名。"}],"wordCount":102}},{"id":"section3-part-b","kind":"writing","title":"Section III Part B 写作","directions":"52. Directions: Write an essay based on the charts below. In your essay, you should 1) describe the charts briefly, 2) interpret the charts, and 3) give your comments. Write your answer in 160-200 words on the ANSWER SHEET. (20 points)","prompt":"52. Directions: Write an essay based on the charts below. In your essay, you should 1) describe the charts briefly, 2) interpret the charts, and 3) give your comments. Write your answer in 160-200 words on the ANSWER SHEET. (20 points)","points":20,"image":{"src":"assets/writing/2026-english-one-section3-part-b.jpg","alt":"该图正下方的文字是“一项关于养老机器人的消费者接受度和首要关注点的调查”","width":"1024","height":"554","local":"assets/writing/2026-english-one-section3-part-b.jpg"},"model":{"essay":"The pie chart and the bar chart above present the findings of a survey on consumer acceptance of elderly-care robots and people's foremost concerns. As is shown in the pie chart, 39.3% of the respondents fully accept such robots, 32.8% partially accept them, and the remaining 27.9% refuse to accept them. Meanwhile, the bar chart indicates that safety ranks first at 46.30%, far ahead of price, which accounts for 24.90%, and convenience, which makes up only 10.70%. Several factors account for these figures. As society ages rapidly and the demand for elderly care keeps rising, robots are increasingly viewed as a practical solution, which explains why over 70% of people accept them to some degree. However, since these machines serve vulnerable elderly users, the public naturally worries most about whether they are safe and reliable rather than cheap or convenient. In my view, elderly-care robots represent a promising trend that deserves encouragement. To win wider acceptance, manufacturers should place safety at the core of their design while gradually bringing down prices. Only when these concerns are properly addressed can such technology truly benefit the aging population.","translation":"上方的饼状图和条形图呈现了一项关于养老机器人消费者接受度以及人们首要关注点的调查结果。饼状图显示，39.3% 的受访者完全接受这类机器人，32.8% 部分接受，其余 27.9% 则不接受。与此同时，条形图表明，安全以 46.30% 高居首位，远超占 24.90% 的价格和仅占 10.70% 的便利。\n造成这些数据的原因有几点。随着社会快速老龄化、养老照护需求不断上升，机器人日益被视为一种现实的解决办法，这也解释了为何超过七成的人在某种程度上愿意接受它们。然而，由于这些机器服务的是脆弱的老年群体，公众自然最担心它们是否安全可靠，而非是否便宜或便利。\n在我看来，养老机器人代表着一种值得鼓励的良好趋势。为赢得更广泛的接受，厂商应把安全放在设计的核心，同时逐步降低价格。只有当这些顾虑被妥善解决，这项技术才能真正惠及老龄人口。","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"总述＋趋势描述","text":"【第 1 段】 总述＋趋势描述 。首句 present the findings of a survey on ... 一句总述交代两图主题，不出现数字；随后 39.3% ... 32.8% ... the remaining 27.9% 分述饼图，safety ranks first at 46.30%, far ahead of price, which accounts for 24.90% 用排名与占比表达描述条形图，落实了 describe the charts briefly，且篇幅偏短。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"由数字追原因","text":"【第 2 段】 由数字追原因 。Several factors account for these figures 过渡领起；As society ages rapidly ... which explains why over 70% ... 把接受度扣到老龄化，However, since these machines serve vulnerable elderly users ... 把安全顾虑扣到服务对象脆弱，落实 interpret the charts。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"亮观点＋条件式收尾","text":"【第 3 段】 亮观点＋条件式收尾 。In my view ... deserves encouragement 亮立场，manufacturers should place safety at the core ... while gradually bringing down prices 给对策，Only when these concerns are properly addressed can ... 倒装条件句收束，落实 give your comments。"}],"wordCount":186}}],"questionCount":45,"objectiveCount":45,"answerTable":{"ans-section1":"完形填空（四选一） Section I （1–20 题） 1 A Still · 2 D dominating · 3 B area · 4 C Combining · 5 B outcomes · 6 C For instance · 7 A test · 8 D before · 9 A explore · 10 D analyze · 11 D customized · 12 D In this way · 13 C concerns · 14 A pressures · 15 B Additionally · 16 C contribute to · 17 C relying · 18 B such as · 19 B appreciation · 20 A replace \n（逐题解析见 完形填空（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A （21–25 题） 21 C · 22 D · 23 A · 24 B · 25 B \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-2":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (2) （26–30 题） 26 D · 27 A · 28 B · 29 C · 30 A \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-3":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (3) （31–35 题） 31 A · 32 A · 33 C · 34 B · 35 D \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-4":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (4) （36–40 题） 36 D · 37 C · 38 B · 39 D · 40 C \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-b":"段落排序 Section II Part B 预给F → 41=B → 42=E → 预给H → 43=A → 预给C → 44=G → 45=D \n（逐题解析见 段落排序解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-c":"英译汉（划线句子翻译） Section II Part C \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 英译汉（划线句子翻译）解析页","ans-section3-part-a":"写作 Section III Part A \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页","ans-section3-part-b":"写作 Section III Part B \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页"},"coverage":{"objective":45,"explained":40,"missingSections":[]}};
